 Automatic stances require verified native bindings in Options → Controls → Stances/Invocations: Ctrl+Shift+1 Balanced, Ctrl+Shift+2 Defense, Ctrl+Shift+3 Divine. The helper reads saved bindings and enables the checkbox only when all match without conflicts. Defense is requested at <=65% HP, Balanced again at >=85%, with Divine invocation. Disable the checkbox for manual choice. Do not bind Ctrl+Shift+F1/F2/F3: KDE desktop shortcuts conflict. The mercenary-only /stance command is never sent.
 At low levels, prioritize a useful weapon, shield/armor, and heal efficiency over dumping all mana into damage. At higher levels, raise damage cadence only as sustain allows. The tool does not claim a simulated best-in-slot DPS rotation; it favors a simple, durable solo setup.
 
-AUTOPILOT (F11): UNATTENDED LEVELING
-EverQuest Legends' Rules of Conduct forbid unattended play; running the autopilot is your own accepted risk. It does nothing to hide itself: fixed timings, no avoidance of players or GMs.
-Before the first run:
-1. Calibrate HP (Combat tab); the autopilot never runs without a live HP reading. A mana calibration adds mana rests.
-2. Keep /log on and the default movement keys: Up arrow moves forward, Left and Right arrows turn (not strafe).
-3. On open, flat ground, click Calibrate movement (Autopilot tab) and switch to the game: the character walks a short zig-zag to measure its speed and turning.
-4. Record the routes ahead of you. Walk each route once and press F12 at the start, at every turn, about every 40 units on straight paths, at the top and bottom of ramps, and right in front of each zone line; then walk through it. The route splits at the zone line and continues from where you arrive. Finish at the camp in the Autopilot tab. A patrol loop and a camp anchor can be recorded there too.
-5. Stand at your camp, or on a recorded route, and press F11. Starting within reach of an unrecorded camp makes that spot its anchor.
-While it runs, it picks the guide's camp for your level, walks there over your routes and zone lines, /targets the camp's mobs by name and accepts only those inside its level window (by default your level minus the larger of 2 and 30%, up to your level + 1). It pulls with your damage spell, fights, lets the client's own auto-loot collect, rests (sits below 70% HP or 50% mana; stands at 98%/95%, after 3 minutes, or at once when hit), refreshes spells after a ding with F6 between fights, and moves on when the camp's band is outgrown. Attackers are always fought back.
-It stops and says why when it is stuck on a route, a route or camp anchor is missing, the next camp needs a boat, translocator or key, a crossing enters the wrong zone, /location gets no reply, HP has no reading for 30 seconds, or F6 fails twice; and on F9, F11, chat, death or an unplanned zone change. Combat assist stays on after an autopilot stop; F9 turns it off. Leaving the game window or pressing your own movement keys only pauses it.
+TRAVEL STATUS
+F11 travel and F12 recording are disabled in this preview. Routes for levels 1–20 are being built from copied client geometry; real movement validation is incomplete. You do not need to record routes by hand.
+The separate --measure-navigation mode offers only the supervised G0 stop check. Passing G0 does not enable leveling or certify a route.
 
 SOURCE OF SPELL LEVELS
 The installed client's spells_us.txt is authoritative for this installation. The progression tab is a preview; your live bar is filtered against a fresh /outputfile spellbook export. No third-class skills are assumed.
